{"case_id": "PyCQA/flake8#1328", "environment_id": "PyCQA__flake8__1328", "note": "Tests an internal implementation detail instead of the reported behaviour. The issue is user-visible: flake8 makes pycodestyle's E111 lose the indent size, so users see 'indentation is not a multiple of ' instead of '... of 4'. The test never runs flake8 or checks the emitted E111 diagnostic; it asserts FileProcessor.indent_size_str == '4'. That field is the cause the fix chose, but the test never demonstrates the broken message a user actually sees.", "reviewed_at": "2026-08-31T14:43:50Z", "reviewer": "Abhishek Sharma", "schema_version": 1, "split": "heldout", "test_patch_sha256": "27d5d0b04d5a543bf8293b7a3e198c7206c3e2054ffff05a1ff0f3483437a4ed", "verdict": "reject"}
{"case_id": "jd/tenacity#586", "environment_id": "jd__tenacity__586", "note": "Tests the type protocol's implementation, not the mypy regression. The reported problem is that passing a normal logging.Logger to before_sleep_log produces a mypy [arg-type] error. The test never invokes mypy; it uses inspect.signature() and asserts one parameter of LoggerProtocol.log is not POSITIONAL_ONLY. Removing that marker is how the fix works, but the meaningful regression test would check that the user's example type-checks.", "reviewed_at": "2026-08-31T14:43:50Z", "reviewer": "Abhishek Sharma", "schema_version": 1, "split": "dev", "test_patch_sha256": "7a04683b0cb39c1db2b44fbbab60d3f0f47d54e45bc0ce63cc0003076f9b18a3", "verdict": "reject"}
{"case_id": "pallets/click#1737", "environment_id": "pallets__click__1737", "note": "Tests the PR's deprecation strategy rather than the issue. The issue says Click's compat shim is unnecessary post-Python-2 and suggests stdlib shutil.get_terminal_size(). It does not report that click.get_terminal_size() should emit a DeprecationWarning, which is the test's distinguishing assertion. The parent fails because it hasn't adopted the chosen migration, not because it exhibits the described problem.", "reviewed_at": "2026-08-31T14:43:50Z", "reviewer": "Abhishek Sharma", "schema_version": 1, "split": "dev", "test_patch_sha256": "ce68b02891970885300f62a65e3acd9efb5146b290e15a3cddd69029ad3c4fcc", "verdict": "reject"}
{"case_id": "pallets/click#1937", "environment_id": "pallets__click__1937", "note": "Does not reproduce shell completion or the reported exception. The issue describes the completion example crashing during shell completion. The test bypasses Click's completion machinery entirely, calls list_users() directly, and requires a concrete list of three users. Returning a generator is presumably the cause, but 'must return a list' is an implementation constraint. A behavioural test would run completion and verify the TypeError is gone.", "reviewed_at": "2026-08-31T14:43:50Z", "reviewer": "Abhishek Sharma", "schema_version": 1, "split": "dev", "test_patch_sha256": "abb27a69b430cfd62de2818b997e3716a23f5af0025cd55290f136ca5901125d", "verdict": "reject"}
{"case_id": "pallets/flask#4342", "environment_id": "pallets__flask__4342", "note": "Tests a deprecation warning the issue never specifies. The issue is an API-cleanup question: g has lived on AppContext for years, so is there a reason to keep RequestContext.g? The test asserts that accessing _request_ctx_stack.top.g emits a DeprecationWarning. Deprecating it was the PR's chosen transition, but the absence of that warning on the parent is not the problem described. Solution-adjacent, not issue-behavioural.", "reviewed_at": "2026-08-31T14:43:50Z", "reviewer": "Abhishek Sharma", "schema_version": 1, "split": "dev", "test_patch_sha256": "3db53ee909f457966285abdacb1a4b6f547fef791507ece9febfa8950283bebc", "verdict": "reject"}
{"case_id": "pallets/flask#4998", "environment_id": "pallets__flask__4998", "note": "Tests deprecation instead of the concurrency bug. The issue says locked_cached_property acquires its lock on every descriptor access, so thread-like workers block each other unnecessarily. The test constructs the property and expects a DeprecationWarning. It never performs repeated access, checks lock acquisition, or creates concurrent workers. The parent fails because the warning wasn't implemented, not because of the reported locking behaviour.", "reviewed_at": "2026-08-31T14:43:50Z", "reviewer": "Abhishek Sharma", "schema_version": 1, "split": "dev", "test_patch_sha256": "1fd6422bff0a5e473efd03b8a813c29f72062a01a5af58a19cc38ffd4f6135a7", "verdict": "reject"}
{"case_id": "psf/black#4447", "environment_id": "psf__black__4447", "note": "Tests the workaround, not the source-corruption bug. The issue reports that on Python 3.12.5 / M1, Black intermittently drops characters from an f-string and raises INTERNAL ERROR: Black produced code that is not equivalent. The test never uses the reproducer. It formats a trivial 'x = 1' while mocking Python 3.12.5 and expects Black to refuse to start. Blocking 3.12.5 is the mitigation the PR chose; the parent formatting 'x = 1' successfully is not the reported failure.", "reviewed_at": "2026-08-31T14:43:50Z", "reviewer": "Abhishek Sharma", "schema_version": 1, "split": "heldout", "test_patch_sha256": "ca0c26a70a64afde3a7d9fb9527477c3c6d28189aa0fdc73ebfa1d8371dc121d", "verdict": "reject"}
{"case_id": "psf/black#4541", "environment_id": "psf__black__4541", "note": "Tests the optimization's implementation rather than the performance regression. The issue says any(token.startswith(p) for p in fstring_prefix) costs too much tokenizer time, with a profiling benchmark. The only assertion distinguishing parent from fix is isinstance(fstring_prefix, tuple). The functional is_fstring_start assertions do not test speed. A tuple is merely what str.startswith(tuple) requires; a meaningful test would exercise tokenization cost.", "reviewed_at": "2026-08-31T14:43:50Z", "reviewer": "Abhishek Sharma", "schema_version": 1, "split": "heldout", "test_patch_sha256": "1fa78bc885de5f4b1e6523a7b5d4a2b3c039b60662b0be4b9475fd9618f4def9", "verdict": "reject"}
